WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.440
So he did a six-week training study

2
00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.320
where he had one cohort of mice training first,

3
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.660
like, you know, about eight, nine

4
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:08.680
in the morning for us,

5
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.560
and another cohort training

6
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.460
at like five o'clock in the afternoon.

7
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.860
And so at the start,

8
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.220
the difference between morning and afternoon runners

9
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.360
is quite large in these mice.

10
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.760
At three weeks, they're both adapting,

11
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.860
they're both getting more increasing endurance.

12
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.440
But the difference between the morning

13
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.980
and the afternoon is still significantly different.

14
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.060
There's still quite a large difference.

15
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.500
But by six weeks, the difference is gone.

16
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.200
So there is something that happens,

17
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.500
it's not an immediate response,

18
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.600
there's something that happens

19
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.800
between the three-week and the six-week timeframe.

20
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.680
So now our morning runners

21
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.080
are performing this at the same level

22
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.720
as the afternoon ones.

23
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.780
For anybody that studies exercise,

24
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.560
we used a workload

25
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.620
that is the same relative workload,

26
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.320
which means that our morning exercisers

27
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.800
were actually doing less absolute work

28
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.790
than the afternoon.

29
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.240
So it was really surprising to us

30
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.800
that we actually saw this enhanced adaptation.

31
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.220
Bottom line,

32
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.260
we looked at a variety of different parameters.

33
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.480
But going back to the muscle clock,

34
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.500
the muscle clocks in those morning runners

35
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.660
shifted in phase by almost four or five hours.

36
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.460
All right.

37
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.620
And what that means is then if you again,

38
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.380
if you think about that mitochondrial function,

39
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.560
what will happen is that should move with it.

40
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.540
So the fact that you have better mitochondrial function

41
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:26.220
in the afternoon

42
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.780
is a direct result

43
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.320
of the clock in the muscle.

44
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:29.820
All right.

45
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.690
So now all we've done with morning training

46
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.300
is we've moved the clock earlier.

47
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.940
So now that mitochondrial performance

48
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.040
is going to be moved

49
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.620
to an earlier part of the day.

50
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.580
And we propose that this is what underlies

51
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.380
that accelerated or enhanced training adaptation

52
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.610
in the morning runners.
